Style Protocol Format Typical use REST HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 JSON / XML Public and internal web APIs — the dominant choice gRPC HTTP/2 Protocol Buffers (binary) High-performance internal service-to-service calls GraphQL HTTP JSON Flexible client-driven queries; Backend for Frontend (BFF) pattern WebSocket WebSocket protocol (starts as an HTTP <code>Upgrade</code>, then full-duplex over TCP) Any Real-time bidirectional communication — chat, live feeds, collaboration Message-based AMQP / Kafka protocol JSON / Avro Asynchronous event-driven integration between services <p>The same operation — "give me product 42" — looks quite different in each of the three request/response styles:</p> RESTgRPCGraphQL <pre><code>GET /products/42 HTTP/1.1
Accept: application/json
</code></pre> <p>The URL identifies the resource and the HTTP method says what to do. Any HTTP client (browser, <code>curl</code>, Postman) can call it.</p> <pre><code>service ProductService {
  rpc GetProduct (GetProductRequest) returns (Product);
}
message GetProductRequest { string id = 1; }
message Product { string id = 1; string name = 2; double price = 3; }
</code></pre> <p>The contract is a <code>.proto</code> file; code is generated for client and server, and messages travel as compact binary. Calling it needs a gRPC client — a browser cannot call it directly.</p> <pre><code>query {
  product(id: "42") { name price }
}
</code></pre> <p>A single endpoint (usually <code>POST /graphql</code>); the client chooses the fields it wants, which avoids over-fetching on mobile screens.</p> <p>How to choose. For the store's public API (browsers, mobile apps, partners), REST is the default: universal tooling, cacheable <code>GET</code>s, easy to debug. gRPC pays off for chatty internal calls where latency and payload size matter. GraphQL fits a front-end that composes data from many services. Messaging is the choice when the producer should not wait for the consumer — covered in Event-Driven Architecture.</p>

Small (1–5 devs)Medium (5–20 devs)Large (20+ devs) <p>Fewer than 10 services? Start here.</p> <pre><code>flowchart TD
    A{"Must it run on AWS?&lt;br/&gt;(credits, compliance)"}
    A --&gt;|No| B{"Users spread over&lt;br/&gt;several continents?"}
    B --&gt;|No| C["Railway / Render&lt;br/&gt;(git push → URL)"]
    B --&gt;|Yes| D["Fly.io&lt;br/&gt;(multi-region machines)"]
    A --&gt;|Yes| E{"One VM or a few&lt;br/&gt;containers is enough?"}
    E --&gt;|Yes| F["Lightsail&lt;br/&gt;(fixed monthly price)"]
    E --&gt;|No| G["ECS Express Mode&lt;br/&gt;(Fargate + ALB)"]</code></pre> <p>Key recommendation: Railway or Render for the fastest time-to-live — connect a GitHub repo and you have a live URL in minutes. On AWS, Lightsail when a fixed, predictable bill matters most; ECS Express Mode when you want a managed container platform that can later grow into full ECS without migrating.</p> <p>EKS at this scale</p> <p>The EKS control plane alone costs $0.10/hour ≈ $73/month<sup>1</sup>. Add a minimum node group (2 × <code>t3.medium</code> ≈ \(61/month), a load balancer and a database, and you are spending around **\)180/month** before any traffic — several times the whole bill on the simpler platforms (see section 5). For a small product team, Kubernetes is not an option here — it is a liability.</p> <p>10–50 services. You need production-grade infrastructure but probably not a full platform engineering team.</p> <pre><code>flowchart TD
    A{"Dedicated ops /&lt;br/&gt;platform engineer?"}
    B["ECS Fargate + ALB + RDS&lt;br/&gt;(serverless containers, no nodes)"]
    C["EKS (managed node groups)&lt;br/&gt;or ECS with more control"]

    A --&gt;|No| B
    A --&gt;|Yes| C</code></pre> <p>Why ECS Fargate for medium teams: no EC2 nodes to patch, and native integration with AWS IAM, Secrets Manager, ECR, and service discovery (Cloud Map / Service Connect). You lose the Kubernetes ecosystem but avoid the fixed control-plane fee and the node operations, which makes day-to-day work dramatically simpler.</p> <p>Fargate does not scale to zero by itself</p> <p>An ECS service keeps its <code>desiredCount</code> of tasks running and billing 24/7. Auto scaling adjusts that count between a minimum and a maximum; reaching zero requires setting the minimum (or a schedule) to 0 yourself, and the next request then has no task to serve it.</p> ECS Fargate EKS Node management None (serverless) You manage node groups (or pay for Auto Mode) Learning curve Low (task definitions) High (full K8s API) Ecosystem (Helm, CRDs) Limited Full Fixed cost before any workload None — pay per task ~$73/month control plane per cluster Multi-cloud portability AWS-only Portable (standard K8s API) GitOps (ArgoCD, Flux) Limited First-class <p>50+ services. EKS is the right answer — now the operational cost is justified.</p> <p>At this scale, you need:</p> <ul> <li>Managed node groups (or Karpenter for bin-packing autoscaling)</li> <li>ArgoCD for GitOps — declarative cluster state from Git</li> <li>AWS Load Balancer Controller — provision ALB/NLB from Kubernetes Ingress resources</li> <li>external-secrets-operator — sync Vault or AWS Secrets Manager secrets into Kubernetes Secrets</li> <li>Separate clusters per environment — dev, staging, prod each get their own EKS cluster</li> </ul> Add-on Purpose Karpenter Node autoscaling — provision exactly the right instance types for pending pods ArgoCD GitOps — declarative cluster state reconciled from Git AWS Load Balancer Controller Provision ALB/NLB from Kubernetes Ingress resources External Secrets Operator Sync Vault/Secrets Manager secrets into Kubernetes Secrets Metrics Server Enables HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler) Cluster Autoscaler Alternative to Karpenter for node scaling <p>EKS Auto Mode</p> <p>Since December 2024, EKS Auto Mode lets AWS manage the worker nodes as well (Karpenter-based provisioning, OS patching) together with core add-ons such as load balancing and block storage, for a management fee on top of the EC2 price<sup>1</sup><sup>9</sup>. It reduces the node work in this list, not the need to understand Kubernetes.</p> <p>Then why does the course project use EKS?</p> <p>The project deploys the store on EKS with a team of 2–3 people — exactly what this class advises a startup not to do. The goal is different: the project exists to learn Kubernetes and AWS, not to minimise cost or time-to-market. Keep the cluster small, delete it when you are not using it, and use the Costs task to compare its bill with the alternatives from section 5.</p>
